  "content": "Goal: track intermittent parsing failures when using function/tool-calling + structured output. Repro is flaky (seen in production traffic and local repros). Notes + commands tried, error snippets, hypotheses, and next steps.\n\nSymptoms observed:\n- Client calls Redwood API with function_call/schema and occasionally gets a parse error when trying to parse model response into expected JSON struct.\n- Error surfaces in SDK as: \"StructuredOutputParseError: invalid JSON payload\" or sometimes \"Unexpected token '}' in JSON at position 412\".\n- When enabled, streaming responses sometimes include raw text chunks before final JSON which breaks naive parser.\n- Failure rate ~0.5-1% on a high-volume customer route; higher under bursty traffic.\n\nExamples (redacted):\n1) Request (JS SDK simplified):\nawait client.complete({\n  model: 'redwood-model-1',\n  input: prompt,\n  function_call: { name: 'extract_order', parameters: { type: 'object', properties: { order_id: { type: 'string' }, items: { type: 'array' } }, required: ['order_id'] } },\n  stream: false\n});\n\n2) Successful model response (expected):\n{\"name\":\"extract_order\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"order_id\\\":\\\"ORD-12345\\\",\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"sku\\\":\\\"A1\\\",\\\"qty\\\":1}]}\"}\n\n3) Failure snippet from logs (raw tokenized capture):\n{\"name\":\"extract_order\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"order_id\\\":\\\"ORD-12345\\\",\\\"items\\\":[{\\\"sku\\\":\\\"A1\\\",\\\"qty\\\":1}]}}\"  <-- extra } at end (double close)\n\n4) Another failure case (streamed chunks):\nchunk1: {\"name\":\"extract_order\",\"arguments\":\"{\\\"order_id\\\":\\\"ORD-123\" \nchunk2: ,\"items\":[]}\"}\nWhen client concatenates chunks, it sometimes inserts newline/whitespace or the SDK's streaming-to-string helper inadvertently adds trailing chars.\n\nCommands & repro attempts:\n- Reproduced locally with recorded prompt from customer: toggled stream true/false, retried 50x. Failure appeared 1/40 runs for stream=true, 0/50 for stream=false.\n- curl (non-streaming):\n  curl -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/complete -H 'Authorization: Bearer $KEY' -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{\"model\":\"redwood-model-1\",\"input\":\"${PROMPT}\",\"function_call\":{...}}'\n  -> response body: valid JSON most times; on failure returned an HTTP 200 with body that parses as JSON but the inner arguments string has malformed JSON.\n- python requests with timeout=30: similar behavior.\n- Inserted extra logging at gateway to capture raw bytes returned by runtime to see if issue originates server-side. Observed in some traces that runtime returns a response where the function arguments string contains additional escape sequences (\\n) or an extra closing bracket.\n\nError examples captured (sanitized):\n- StructuredOutputParseError: invalid JSON payload at index 0\n- JSONDecodeError: Unterminated string starting at: line 1 column 412 (char 411)\n- SDK: \"failed to parse function arguments: expected object but got string\" (meaning the model returned a string containing JSON, but that JSON was itself invalid)\n\nHypotheses:\n1) Model output variance: model sometimes outputs a trailing sentence or punctuation after the JSON blob (e.g., \"}\" followed by explanatory text), causing parser failure.\n2) Streaming chunk boundary issues: chunk concatenation logic in SDK or gateway may be inserting extra characters or dropping escape chars.\n3) Double-encoding/escaping: runtime or earlier layer may be serializing the arguments string twice (i.e., escaping quotes twice) leading to mismatched braces after unescape.\n4) Model sampling/temperature: under higher temperature, model may hallucinate extra characters. Observed failures correlate slightly with non-zero temperature.\n5) Post-processing step (formatters): older pipeline had a legacy sanitizer that tried to pretty-print candidate JSON and could introduce trailing commas/extra braces under certain edge cases.\n6) Customer prompts with unbalanced braces inside natural language or example code that confuse the model leading to malformed JSON in arguments.\n\nLogs / traces to inspect:\n- Gateway raw response bytes (capture at TCP level for failed requests).\n- Runtime request/response logs (model output tokens).\n- SDK client logs (streaming buffer behavior).\n- Any pre/post processors that touch the function_call field.\n\nThings tried so far:\n- Tightened the JSON unescape routine in the SDK to be more defensive: attempt JSON.parse, fallback to heuristic fixes (trim trailing non-json chars, try unescape sequences). Reduced app-level errors but this is bandaid.\n- Forced model to return code block fenced JSON (```json ... ```). This reduced error rate but some customers can't change prompts. Also model sometimes includes language before/after fences.\n- Added strict validation on gateway: if arguments string fails to parse, write raw token sequence and model logits (for later analysis). Implemented for a small % of traffic.\n- Lowered temperature for test runs: failure rate decreased but not eliminated.\n- Added retry-on-parse-fail with fresh call to model using clarifying prompt (extracted original prompt + instruction: \"return only the JSON for function call\"). This helped but increases latency/cost.\n\nOpen questions / next steps:\n- Can runtime include a dedicated metadata envelope instead of embedding arguments as a string that then needs additional parsing? E.g., return arguments as a structured JSON field rather than string-encoded JSON. (This would require contract change between model output parsing and runtime.)\n- Investigate whether the tokenizer/byte-level transforms in our runtime are corrupting escape sequences when joining token chunks into strings. Capture token-level outputs for failing traces.\n- Add more robust streaming concatenation test cases in SDK (simulate mid-token splits, embedded escaped quotes, multi-byte UTF-8 sequences).\n- Consider model-level constraint: add a safety post-processor step to validate JSON structure and attempt safe corrections (remove trailing commas, strip explanatory suffixes inside quotes). Need to evaluate reliability and risk of silent corruption.\n- Explore prompting convention: require function responses to be wrapped in a unique sentinel marker we strip server-side (unlikely to be backward-compatible but strong).\n\nImmediate action items (TODOs):\n- [ ] Capture 100 failed traces with full token logs (gateway + runtime) - assigned to Marco.\n- [ ] Add unit tests in SDK for streaming concatenation edge cases - assigned to Riley.\n- [ ] Prototype server-side replacement: return parsed arguments as native JSON instead of string (spike in runtime repo) - assigned to me (Aisha).\n- [ ] Run A/B (10% traffic) enabling fallback retry-on-parse-fail to measure latency/cost impact - coordinate with SRE.\n\nNotes-to-self:\n- Keep fallback retry logic opt-in via config flag per-customer to avoid surprising behavior.\n- When capturing traces, redact PII and tokens. Store only token ids + logits + reconstructed outputs.\n- Follow up with product: see if we can add an API contract change for function_call responses in next major (breaking) version; cross-team discussion required.\n\nUseful snippets: (helpers used during debugging)\n- JS heuristic cleaner used locally:\nfunction tryHeal(jsonStr) {\n  try { return JSON.parse(jsonStr); } catch (e) {\n    // trim trailing non-json chars\n    const m = jsonStr.match(/(^.*\\}+)/s);\n    if (m) { try { return JSON.parse(m[1]); } catch (e2) {} }\n    // try remove last char and parse repeatedly (ugly but useful for debugging)\n    let s = jsonStr;\n    while (s.length > 0) {\n      s = s.slice(0, -1);\n      try { return JSON.parse(s); } catch (e3) { continue; }\n    }\n    throw e;\n  }\n}\n\n- Minimal curl to reproduce + save raw bytes:\ncurl -i -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/complete \\\n  -H 'Authorization: Bearer $KEY' \\\n  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d @payload.json -o raw_response.txt --trace-ascii curl_trace.txt\n\nFinal thought: this feels like a mix of model variance + brittle escaping/streaming handling. Need both better server-side contracts (prefer structured arguments over stringified JSON) and improved SDK defensiveness. Capture token-level traces for failing examples to attribute blame (model vs runtime).\n",
